# ops/anonymize: how the nightly snapshot turns production data into safe data.
# The server applies these rules from production's current release, then checks that no
# real-looking personal data is left, and refuses to store the dump otherwise.
# Every column whose name looks personal needs a rule or an explicit ignore (the PII guard
# in ci enforces this for new migrations).
#
# One directive per line, |-separated; lines starting with # are comments:
#   rule|<table>|<column>|<strategy>[|<arg>]
#   ignore|<table>|<column>|<reason>
#   devlogin|<table>|<key column>|<key value>|<col>=<value>[;<col>=<value>]
#
# Strategies are deterministic (the same input always gets the same fake value, salted per
# project on the server), so relations and unique keys still hold:
#   email       u<10 hex>@example.invalid
#   name, first_name, last_name, address
#   phone       +1555<7 digits>
#   text        fixed lorem ipsum
#   null        NULL
#   redact      <arg>, or REDACTED
#   hash        hex digest
# <table> may be * only in ignore lines. Fake emails may only use example.invalid,
# example.com, example.org, example.net or *.test.
#
# devlogin keeps one known login working on sanitized copies: in the row where
# <key column> = <key value>, it sets the listed columns (for example a known email and
# the password hash your stack expects for the dev password).
#
# Example for a small customers table and a users table (replace with your schema):
# rule|customers|full_name|name
# rule|customers|email|email
# rule|customers|phone|phone
# rule|customers|address|address
# rule|customers|birth_date|null
# rule|customers|notes|text
# rule|users|email|email
# rule|users|remember_token|null
# rule|payments|card_last4|redact|0000
# ignore|customers|country_code|not personal on its own
# ignore|*|created_at|timestamps are not personal
# devlogin|users|id|1|email=dev@example.test;name=Dev Login
